{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## A THIRD DEFECT, and it is the real fail-open — reproduced deterministically\n\nRe-ran the verifier standalone against e369 with full capture. It reproduces every time:\n\n    32x  WARNING llm call [epic-bug-screen] mode=single_turn\n         model=bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0 FAILED in 0.4s: 400\n         'The maximum tokens you requested exceeds the model limit of 64000'\n    ...\n    {\"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"findings\": [], ... \"model\": \"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\"}\n    exit=0\n\n**Thirty-two failures, and a PASS.** The step is `epic_bug_screen.py:170 _row`:\n\n    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — degrade open per candidate; the close never fails here\n        logger.warning(\"epic bug screen call failed for %s; degrading to C (unrelated)\", ...)\n        verdict = dict(_DEGRADE)\n\n`_DEGRADE` is verdict **C — 'unrelated'**, the most permissive outcome. So each bug that cannot be screened is silently declared unrelated to the epic.\n\n### Why the three defects compose into something worse than any of them\n\n 1. **Problem 1** (no re-clamp when the model shrinks) makes the haiku screen call fail — not sometimes, but **100% of the time**, deterministically.\n 2. **Problem 3** converts each failure into 'unrelated'. Per-candidate degradation is a deliberate, defensible choice; it was never designed for the case where the failure is SYSTEMATIC.\n 3. Result: the epic's caused_by bug floor — the check that asks 'did this epic's own work break something still open?' — **evaluated nothing at all**, and said so nowhere. Not in the verdict, not in the sidecar, not in the exit code.\n\nThat is a safety check that disappears exactly when its dependency is misconfigured, which is the same shape as the enrich-drain push failure earlier tonight: a subsystem reporting success while doing nothing.\n\n**Note this is EPIC-SPECIFIC**, which explains the whole puzzle. `epic-bug-screen` runs only for epics, so the seven bug closes tonight never touched it and all certified normally. Only e369 hit it.\n\n### Classification\n\n`defect`, but the REMEDY is a design decision I am not making unilaterally. The per-candidate degrade is intentional and cited in the code. What is missing is an **aggregate** signal: when every candidate degrades (or some threshold does), the gate should either fail closed or record 'screen unavailable — N candidates unevaluated' on the verdict, so a reader can tell 'no related bugs found' from 'the screen never ran'. Options worth weighing: fail closed on total degradation; carry an `unevaluated` count into the verdict and sidecar; or keep degrading but refuse to CERTIFY when the screen was blind.\n\nRecording rather than fixing, because changing when an epic close blocks is a gate-semantics change that deserves an explicit ruling."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785856638945649001,"uuid":"459f414e-c522-4628-a15d-213f0ce11dda"}